          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Realism in Theatre</strong><br>\nIbsen is known for introducing realism into modern drama. He focused on everyday problems and the inner realities of his characters, challenging the melodrama of the previous theatrical traditions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Peer Gynt\" and Poetry</strong><br>\nWhile Ibsen is primarily known for his plays, he also wrote \"Peer Gynt,\" a five-act play in verse, which showcases his use of Norwegian folklore and his mastery of the poetic form.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Themes of Morality and Society</strong><br>\nHis works often dealt with issues of morality and challenged societal norms, including \"Ghosts,\" which discusses the repercussions of hiding the truth, and \"An Enemy of the People,\" which portrays the consequences of going against the majority.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
